          "passage": "Stilt houses are traditional dwellings found commonly among ethnic minority groups in the mountainous regions of northern and central Viet Nam. These unique houses are built above the ground on sturdy wooden or bamboo poles. Raising the house above the ground provides several important advantages. First, it protects the family from dangerous wild animals and sudden flash floods during the heavy rainy season. Second, the elevated wooden floor keeps the living space cool and well-ventilated during hot summer days. The area underneath the floor is often used for storing farming tools, firewood, or weaving looms. A traditional stilt house usually has a large central living room with an open hearth where the family gathers to cook meals and tell folk stories. Today, even as modern brick houses appear in rural villages, many communities proudly preserve their beautiful stilt houses as symbols of cultural identity."
