          "passage": "The Tay ethnic minority people in Ha Giang usually build their homes on high wooden stilts. This unique style of architecture has existed for centuries. A typical stilt house has two levels. The upper level is the living space for the whole family, while the ground level underneath is used for storing farming tools, firewood, and weaving looms. The framework of the house is made of solid ironwood, and the steep roof is covered with palm leaves to drain heavy rainwater quickly. Inside the house, there is a central hearth that never goes out. It keeps the house warm during cold mountain winters and serves as a gathering spot where elders tell folk tales to children. Visiting a Tay stilt house allows travellers to experience the authentic warmth and hospitality of highlanders."
